Very well; let us endeavour, with due deliberation, to unravel this question. The matter at hand concerns the precise instant at which a Vietnamese bank ought to dispatch a refusal message in the event that discrepancies are discovered in documents tendered under a letter of credit; the particulars supplied inform us that the presentation occurred upon the 30th day of March, 2020, at the hour of 5:01 in the afternoon, whereas the ordinary hours during which the bank conducts its affairs are from 7:30 in the morning until 5:00 in the afternoon, and that the two days commonly known as Saturday and Sunday are held to be non-banking days.

First, it is necessary to summon to mind the rule which governs the period within which a bank must apprise the presenter of any discrepancies in respect of documents under a letter of credit. From memory one recalls that, under the UCP 600, which stands as the customary international canon in this domain, the bank is allowed a maximum period of seven banking days within which to examine the documents and to notify the presenter of any defects; yet one must acknowledge with care that the precise commencement of that interval may be contingent upon the specific terms of the credit and upon the definition of banking days in the jurisdiction where the bank is situated.

Thus the salient particulars are these:

1. The documents were tendered on the 30th of March, 2020, at 5:01 pm; since the bank’s stated hours terminate at 5:00 pm, this presentation occurred but a single minute after the hour of closing. One must pause and ask whether that circumstance bears upon the question of when the documents are to be regarded as received; if presentation occurs after the banking hours, does it not follow that the effective receipt will be ascribed to the next banking day?

Indeed, this consideration commends itself to acceptance. If the bank is closed at the hour of 5:00 pm, then any documents presented subsequent to that hour would reasonably be deemed not to have been received until the next banking day; in the present instance the date of presentation was Monday, the 30th of March, and the next calendar day is Tuesday, the 31st of March; yet, because the presentation took place at 5:01 pm, that is to say after the bank’s working hours, it is plausible, and indeed most consistent with practice, that the documents are to be treated as having been received upon the following business day, namely the 31st of March, 2020.

But one must not proceed without confirmation of the weekday: March 30, 2020, was indeed a Monday. Consequently the subsequent banking day is Tuesday, March 31. Thus it appears that the bank would not have been able properly to accept and to examine the documents until Tuesday, the 31st, because the presentation at 5:01 pm on Monday falls outside the appointed hours of business.

The question therefore reduces to the moment from which the seven banking days commence. According to Article 14 of the UCP 600, the bank must examine the documents within seven banking days from the day on which the documents are received. Hence if the effective day of receipt is the 31st of March, the seven banking days must be counted beginning upon that date. This proposition merits firm assent.

Accordingly, let us enumerate the banking days in question, taking into account that in Vietnam Saturday and Sunday are not banking days, and thus must be excluded from the count. One may note the calendar in the following manner:

March 31 (Tuesday) — day 1;

April 1 (Wednesday) — day 2;

April 2 (Thursday) — day 3;

April 3 (Friday) — day 4;

April 4 (Saturday) — non-banking day, therefore excluded;

April 5 (Sunday) — non-banking day, therefore excluded;

April 6 (Monday) — day 5;

April 7 (Tuesday) — day 6;

April 8 (Wednesday) — day 7.

Thus, upon this computation, the seventh banking day ensuing from the day of receipt, if that day is to be taken as the 31st of March, falls upon the 8th of April, 2020, and it follows that the latest permissible day upon which the bank must despatch its refusal message is the 8th of April, which corresponds to the option marked E.

One must, however, entertain and then set aside an alternative hypothesis, which would suppose that the documents are to be regarded as having been received upon the 30th of March notwithstanding that the tender occurred at 5:01 pm, that is to say a minute after the declared hour of closing. If that contention were to be urged, the seven banking days would commence from the 30th of March. Yet this position is strained, for the bank, having ceased to transact business at 5:00 pm, could not in truth have examined the papers until the next business day; therefore the appropriate and practical determination is that the effective receipt is on the 31st.

This matter is somewhat ambiguous in theory, and one may observe with candour that the UCP speaks of the day of receipt without intruding upon the hour at which the documents arrived; nevertheless, in practice and in fairness, documents presented subsequent to the hour at which the bank closes are treated as received on the next banking day, and we may therefore prefer that interpretation.

Returning to our enumeration from March 31, the seven banking days are, as previously set forth: March 31 (1), April 1 (2), April 2 (3), April 3 (4), April 6 (5), April 7 (6), April 8 (7). On this account the final date by which the refusal message must be communicated is the 8th of April, 2020, and thus the correct selection is, manifestly, option E.

Let us endeavour to unravel, with as much care as the subject demands, this little problem of heredity: we are presented with a population of garden peas, which at the outset is in genetic equilibrium, and wherein the phenotypic frequencies are recorded as full pods (F) 0.6 and constricted pods (f) 0.4; thereafter the plants commence to fertilize themselves for three successive generations, and we are required to deduce the distribution of genotypes after those three generations have elapsed.

In the first instance one recalls the familiar Hardy–Weinberg proportions, by which, under the conditions of random mating and equilibrium, the genotype frequencies are p squared, 2pq, and q squared; here, with p equal to 0.6 and q equal to 0.4, one therefore obtains initially FF: 0.36, Ff: 0.48, ff: 0.16. It is, however, most curious and worthy of note that, although the population is so described as being in equilibrium at the commencement, the subsequent practice of self-fertilization is manifestly not equivalent to random mating, so that the Hardy–Weinberg assumptions do not any longer govern the progeny.

We must, then, consider with care the consequences of self-fertilization: when an individual plant is heterozygous Ff, the gametes which it produces are divided equally between F and f, so that when it fertilizes itself the possible zygotes arise in the proportions FF one quarter, Ff one half, and ff one quarter; whereas when the plant is homozygous FF every gamete bears F and therefore every offspring is FF, and similarly when the plant is homozygous ff every gamete bears f and therefore every offspring is ff. From this it follows, and we may note it with some emphasis, that self-fertilization tends steadily to diminish the frequency of heterozygotes as generations proceed.

With these principles before us, let us proceed, generation by generation, to compute the changes in frequency, taking the initial state as generation 0.

Generation 0 (initial): FF = 0.36, Ff = 0.48, ff = 0.16.

Proceeding to generation 1, and treating each class as self-fertilizing, we compute the contributions thus: for the FF individuals, all their offspring are FF and hence their contribution to FF in generation 1 is 0.36; for the Ff individuals, selfing yields 25% FF, 50% Ff, and 25% ff, so that the contribution from the heterozygotes is 0.48 × 0.25 = 0.12 to FF, 0.48 × 0.5 = 0.24 to Ff, and 0.48 × 0.25 = 0.12 to ff; for the ff individuals, all offspring are ff and their contribution to ff is 0.16. Summing these parts, one obtains for generation 1: FF = 0.36 (from FF) + 0.12 (from Ff) = 0.48; Ff = 0.24 (from Ff); ff = 0.16 (from ff) + 0.12 (from Ff) = 0.28. One may check that these numbers sum to unity: 0.48 + 0.24 + 0.28 = 1.0, which they indeed do.

Advancing to generation 2, we take as our starting proportions the results just obtained, namely FF = 0.48, Ff = 0.24, ff = 0.28, and apply the same mode of reproduction. The FF individuals (0.48) produce only FF offspring, contributing 0.48 to FF; the Ff individuals (0.24) produce, by the proportions above, 0.24 × 0.25 = 0.06 FF, 0.24 × 0.5 = 0.12 Ff, and 0.24 × 0.25 = 0.06 ff; the ff individuals (0.28) produce only ff, contributing 0.28 to ff. Hence, totalling these contributions, generation 2 comprises FF = 0.48 + 0.06 = 0.54, Ff = 0.12, ff = 0.28 + 0.06 = 0.34. A check of their sum gives 0.54 + 0.12 + 0.34 = 1.0, which is as it should be.

Finally, to reach generation 3, we commence with generation 2's frequencies, FF = 0.54, Ff = 0.12, ff = 0.34, and perform the same calculation: the FF fraction (0.54) yields only FF offspring, contributing 0.54 to FF; the Ff fraction (0.12) yields 0.12 × 0.25 = 0.03 FF, 0.12 × 0.5 = 0.06 Ff, and 0.12 × 0.25 = 0.03 ff; the ff fraction (0.34) yields only ff, contributing 0.34 to ff. Upon summation, generation 3 therefore exhibits FF = 0.54 + 0.03 = 0.57, Ff = 0.06, ff = 0.34 + 0.03 = 0.37. A final verification shows that 0.57 + 0.06 + 0.37 = 1.0, and thus the arithmetic is consistent.

Let us now compare these results with the answer choices presented: the options are as follows —

A) 0.54FF + 0.12Ff + 0.34ff — which, as one will observe, corresponds to generation 2 rather than to generation 3;

B) 0.42FF + 0.36Ff + 0.22ff;

C) 0.30FF + 0.40Ff + 0.30ff;

D) 0.48FF + 0.24Ff + 0.28ff — which is plainly generation 1;

E) 0.36FF + 0.48Ff + 0.16ff — the initial generation;

F) 0.57FF + 0.06Ff + 0.37ff — which accords exactly with the calculation for generation 3 that we have just performed;

G) 0.25FF + 0.50Ff + 0.25ff;

H) 0.6FF + 0.2Ff + 0.2ff;

I) 0.64FF + 0.16Ff + 0.20ff;

J) 0.49FF + 0.02Ff + 0.49ff.

Hence, with due confidence and having verified the arithmetic, one selects option F, since 0.57FF + 0.06Ff + 0.37ff is precisely the distribution obtained for generation 3.

Let us endeavour, with calm reflection, to discern the truth of this little puzzle; the question declares that both sides were presenting their arguments as fact, yet plainly they were merely voicing an what? The alternatives set before us are: opinion, eat cake, untrue, belief, or factfile.

First, one must apprehend the context; the salient portion avows that they were presenting their arguments as fact, whereas in reality they were not facts at all. Thus the interrogative seeks to know what, in truth, they were expressing in lieu of facts.

The word "voicing" in this connexion most naturally signifies the act of expressing or stating one s views. If they were not asserting facts but rather their own points of view, what term among the offered alternatives best describes that mode of utterance?

One observes that the terms "opinion" and "belief" lie near to one another in meaning; nevertheless, the sentence inquires whether they were presenting their arguments as fact, but were in truth merely voicing an—what? Hence it is plausible that they were declaring their opinions or their beliefs as if they were facts.

Examining the alternatives with care, we note that "opinion" is letter A and "belief" is letter D. Which, then, fits more suitably? The locution "voicing an opinion" is in common use, as when one speaks of "voicing an opinion on the matter." In like manner one might speak of "voicing a belief."

Yet observe the grammatical hint: the original sentence employs the article "an," which suggests that the subsequent word commences with a vowel sound. Let us therefore scrutinise the options in that light:

A) opinion — commences with a vowel sound (o);
B) eat cake — is described as commencing with a consonant (e);
C) untrue — is described as commencing with a consonant (u);
D) belief — commences with a consonant (b);
E) factfile — commences with a consonant (f).

This consideration leads one to favour A) opinion, for "an opinion" accords both with common idiom and with the grammatical signal furnished by the article.

Nevertheless, one more reflection: were we to entertain "belief" as D, we must note that the phrase would employ "a belief," not "an," and thus does not harmonise with the article originally used. Therefore, with these reasons combined, the answer should be A.
